{
  "_comment": "shex.js's own manifest terms, stacked onto the ShEx manifest vocabulary's context (https://www.w3.org/ns/shex-manifest.jsonld). That vocabulary holds what manifests of ShEx validations have in common, whoever wrote them -- entries, name, an entry's schema, data and queryMap with their ...URL and ...Label spellings, comment, and node, shape and status from the ShEx vocabulary -- and is shared with the conformance suite's manifests (shexTest's, which stack the ShEx test vocabulary, https://www.w3.org/ns/shex-test.jsonld, on it the same way) and with other implementations' examples. What is here is what only shex.js means, and its namespace is this document's own URL, https://shex.js.org/doc/manifest-context.jsonld#, so a term's IRI resolves to its definition. A manifest (packages/*/examples/manifest.yaml, aggregated into doc/tests-manifest.yaml; read by validate, the web app and tools/manifest-runner.js through @shexjs/manifest) stacks the two, then binds the scopes it uses. A manifest with no @context at all is a deployed classic one, and is read as though it stacked both. A neighborhood's or an extension's attributes are LEXICALLY SCOPED: the entry writes them under one `<prefix>:parms` key whose value is a mapping (map:parms, nsp:parms), bare inside it, and the manifest's own @context binds that key -- a prefix of the manifest's choosing and, beside it, the term, whose JSON-LD 1.1 property-scoped @context is the vocabulary's scope file: packages/neighborhood-*/manifest-context.jsonld in this repository, https://shexspec.github.io/extensions/*/manifest-context.jsonld beside each extension's spec in shexSpec/extensions. All of them are also in @shexjs/manifest's static roll-up (packages/shex-manifest/known-contexts.json, refreshed by tools/rollup-manifest-contexts.js), which every reader consults before the network, so a known vocabulary costs no fetch. A scope file defines only the names written inside its mapping, each mapped to an absolute IRI, and declares no prefix, so vocabularies cannot collide and the prefix is the manifest's, written once per entry rather than once per attribute; outside its mapping a bare name is undefined and dropped, and inside it the outer names -- the shared vocabulary and this file -- stay visible, as in any lexical scope, which is how pluginURL is written inside a mapping without the scope file defining it, and how a scope file that wants an attribute another vocabulary defined binds the same bare name to that vocabulary's IRI: the IRI is the identity, the name is local. The binding has to name the TERM (map:parms), not just the prefix: JSON-LD attaches a scoped context to the key as written, so a prefix alone gives map:parms its IRI but not its scope, and the mapping's contents are dropped. In RDF the entry gets one <prefix>:parms arc to a blank node holding them (doc/tests-manifest-ld.ttl shows the graph). pluginURL names a module the web app loads by URL to provide what the entry needs -- a semantic-action extension, a neighborhood, a screen, any or all of these (doc/plugins.md) -- and is written inside the mapping of the vocabulary that module implements, so an entry's plugins sit with their parameters and are all loaded before the entry is read; it replaces the entry-level plugins list. neighborhood names one of the web app's built-in data sources; a plugin-delivered one would come from a pluginURL. dataBase is the base IRI the entry's data and query map resolve against; slurp, the web app's record-what-was-fetched switch. An overlay (overlay/overlayURL: a document of semantic actions applied to the schema, @shexjs/semact-overlay) is ShExReduce's, so it is written in that extension's scope, rdc:parms, and defined by https://shexspec.github.io/extensions/Reduce/manifest-context.jsonld. manifestContext is the one term no entry writes: the RDF rendering of a manifest (tools/yamlld-to-nested-turtle.js) says with it which scope file defines each vocabulary -- <namespace> shexjs:manifestContext <url>, one triple per binding the manifest's @context declares -- because a JSON-LD context is not part of the graph, and without it a reader of the graph could not tell whether an IRI-valued attribute is spelled outputSchemaURL or endpoint. explain, regexpEngine and meta[URL] are here because deployed manifests carry them: every key the web app's gists have ever written (its input registry's manifest descriptors) and every key it reads back (a term-resolver graph) is a term of one of the two contexts, so reading a manifest as JSON-LD loses nothing the app would have used. An entry the app lists without a schemaLabel or a dataLabel is labeled by its name, the shared vocabulary's; a classic manifest's title, which the app used to read for that, is read as its name. NOTE: this top-level _comment sits beside @context, not inside it -- a JSON-LD context's own term values may only be strings or objects, never arrays, so this is deliberately here instead.",

  "@context": {
    "shexjs": "https://shex.js.org/doc/manifest-context.jsonld#",

    "neighborhood": "shexjs:neighborhood",
    "pluginURL": {"@id": "shexjs:plugin", "@type": "@id"},
    "manifestContext": {"@id": "shexjs:manifestContext", "@type": "@id"},

    "dataBase": {"@id": "shexjs:dataBase", "@type": "@id"},
    "slurp": "shexjs:slurp",

    "explain": "shexjs:explain",
    "regexpEngine": "shexjs:regexpEngine",
    "meta": "shexjs:meta",
    "metaURL": {"@id": "shexjs:meta", "@type": "@id"}
  }
}
